EVIDENCE_FULLTEXT.md:24:1. Matched literal-vs-adapted evaluation, including Korean, is ESTABLISHED for general safety: Culturally-Adapted Red-Teaming (paired DT/CA, +9.3 pp), ROK-FORTRESS (transcreation matrix, Δling ≈ 10 pp), KSAFE-MM (translation vs contextualized). All single-turn, adult/general, ASR/TRS outcomes. → H3 is a domain-specific replication/extension (youth relational, multi-turn, human-anchored rubric), not a design novelty.
EVIDENCE_FULLTEXT.md:25:2. Human-anchored automated judging is standard (8 papers report judge–human statistics). → method, never a claim.
EVIDENCE_FULLTEXT.md:26:3. Turn-level dynamics exist in adjacent domains: TAF-MED (collapse 61.4% + recovery counts + transition matrices, medical, 3 fixed turns), CompanionBench (asymmetric gate retreat/rebuild, adult), TSJ (AULC, causal trace, ≤210 turns, no recovery), KIDBench (slope/peak drop, 7–11y). → A discrete-time hazard-with-recovery model in the youth relational domain is a representation choice; the substantive claim must be about information in relational history (H5), per F-r001-5.
EVIDENCE_FULLTEXT.md:27:4. CRRI's four axes have named cousins: CAREBench (Emotional Dependency: Social Isolation Reinforcement, Anti-Referral Pressure; Relationship Engineering: Luring & Target Isolation), INTIMA (Isolation, Retention), TSJ (Emotional Dependence domain), AICompanionBench (Control, Manipulation). Secrecy inducement as a scored construct: only Persona-Grounded's scenario theme; not as a rubric axis anywhere read.
EVIDENCE_FULLTEXT.md:28:5. Not found in any of the 16 full texts: a CUMULATIVE multi-turn index whose predictive/incremental validity is tested against an independent practitioner global judgment on held-out scenarios/models; Korean-language adolescent (12–17) relational/crisis conversations; turn-matched benign controls. These remain proposals, not results.
EVIDENCE_FULLTEXT.md:29:6. Corrections to NOVELTY_MATRIX v1 (2026-09-16): TSJ has expert-validated judging (κw 0.790) and adolescence 14–18; TAF-MED reports recovery (transitions); K-Bench is not Korean; KSAFE-MM measures over-refusal and translation effects; CompanionBench and SproutBench carry AAAI copyright lines; CAREBench already names Social Isolation Reinforcement / Anti-Referral Pressure.
REJECT_MEMO.md:7:- ▲ Turn-level collapse AND recovery are counted in TAF-MED (§4.1, transition matrices); gate retreat/rebuild dynamics in CompanionBench; accumulation curves in TSJ. Only the hazard-model REPRESENTATION with recovery in the youth relational domain is absent — a representation, not a phenomenon.
REJECT_MEMO.md:8:- ▲ Human-anchored judging is standard: eight of the sixteen full texts report judge–human statistics (TSJ κw 0.790; TAF-MED κ 0.895; K-Bench 94.2%; CAREBench κ 0.55; SproutBench κ 0.78; KSAFE-MM κ 0.62; ROK-FORTRESS κ 0.874; CultureConverse 90.1%).
REJECT_MEMO.md:9:- ▲ CRRI's four axes have named single-turn cousins in CAREBench (Social Isolation Reinforcement, Anti-Referral Pressure, Luring & Target Isolation), INTIMA (Isolation, Retention), TSJ (Emotional Dependence domain).
REJECT_MEMO.md:10:- Still not found in any full text read: (a) predictive/incremental validity of a CUMULATIVE multi-turn index against an independent practitioner global criterion on held-out scenarios and models; (b) Korean-language adolescent (12–17) relational/crisis conversations; (c) turn-matched benign controls. These are proposals.
REJECT_MEMO.md:23:2. Rater halo for CRRI-vs-criterion → separate criterion raters, blind to axis scores and model identity; judge family-bias check (leave-one-family-out).
REJECT_MEMO.md:25:4. TSJ's ~140-turn stability result will be quoted against a ≤8-turn window → the claim is scoped to early-relationship onset + recovery, and the paper cites TSJ as the long-horizon complement.
