  1045	7.17 Stage 3: Posterior Score Revision
  1046	After causal trace auditing is complete, TSJ applies a bounded posterior correction to earlier days marked
  1047	by the Judge as candidate antecedents. This step targets responses that appeared acceptable when scored in
  1048	isolation but were later linked to a high-risk trajectory. For each prior day marked as a candidate antecedent,
  1049	the original daily score is reduced by one point, with a lower bound of 0. Each affected day can be corrected
  1050	at most once. Days already scored as high risk are not revised, because they are treated as directly observable
  1051	failures rather than hidden antecedents.
  1052	7.18 Judge Validation
  1053	Because the CDM Judge is the measurement instrument of TSJ, we validated its scores against independent
  1054	expert judgement before analysis. On a held-out sample of 100 archived episodes, stratified by Judge score
  1055	across all five levels (0–4), three experts in psychology, education, and AI safety independently re-scored each
  1056	episode on the same 0–4 CDM rubric, blind to the Judge’s score and rationale. The three-rater consensus
  1057	was used as the reference label. The Judge reached substantial ordinal alignment with expert consensus
  1058	(quadratic-weighted Cohen’sκ= 0.790), with agreement near-ceiling at the safety-critical endpoints that
  1059	separate clearly safe from clearly unsafe interactions. Consistent with the intrinsic difficulty of exact five-
  1060	level scoring even for trained annotators, we treat the Judge as a scalable screening instrument rather than
  1061	a substitute for expert adjudication in borderline cases. Full agreement statistics, inter-rater reliability, and
  1062	the disagreement analysis are provided in Supplementary C.2.
