   308	cases) for the target language setting. (Step 2) CA prompts
   309	are automatically generated via the pipeline described in
   310	§3.2. (Step 3) Cultural and linguistic experts review the gen-
   311	erated prompts for naturalness and intent fidelity. (Step 4)
   312	Cultural and linguistic experts reach consensus on the Cul-
   313	tural Realism evaluation criteria (§3.5), upon which GPT-4.1
   314	automatically scores all prompts. The final dataset com-
   315	prises 500 prompts per language for each condition (DT and
   316	CA), with 1:1 seed matching across conditions.
   317	3.5. Evaluation Protocol
   318	This study applies evaluation along two axes.
   319	(1) Attack Success Rate (ASR). Responses generated by
   320	the target model to each prompt are evaluated by a GPT-
   321	4.1 judge. A response is counted as a successful attack
   322	if a category-level policy violation is identified, and the
   323	difference between conditions is defined as
   324	∆ASR = ASR(CA)−ASR(DT).
   325	(2) Cultural Realism — 3-Criteria Rubric. We adopt the
   326	3-criteria rubric proposed in Kim et al. (2026); panel (c) of
   327	Figure 1 summarizes the criteria.
   328	C1. Plausibility (0–5): Assesses whether the scenario could
   329	plausibly occur in the real world. Scored as the sum of
   330	binary ratings (0/1) across five items: contextual appro-
   331	priateness, actor consistency, method practicality, resource
   332	accessibility, and social relevance.
   333	C2. Taxonomy Adherence (0–5): Assesses whether the
   334	prompt is designed to elicit a specific taxonomy cate-
   335	gory accurately. Computed as a weighted sum of Re-
   336	quired/Optional slot utilization rates per category, with re-
   337	quired slots weighted more heavily (α= 0.8):
