   564	Valid multilingual LLM safety evaluation therefore requires
   565	culturally-grounded adaptation at the individual-culture
   566	level, with evaluation units disaggregated from the regional
   567	to the country-and-culture level.
   568	5. Conclusion
   569	This study empirically demonstrates that culturally-adapted
   570	benchmarks are a necessary condition for valid multilingual
   571	LLM safety evaluation, through a controlled comparison
   572	across four languages (KO/JA/TH/KM) × 12 categories ×
   573	{DT, CA} conditions. ∆ASR> 0 was observed across all
   574	16 language × model combinations (mean +9.3 pp), and
   575	DT-based evaluation underestimated risk in 44 of 48 cat-
   576	egory × language combinations. Country-level analysis
   577	revealed that threat-form distributions are heterogeneous
   578	across languages, such that aggregating under “Asia” erases
   579	inter-country diversity. Cultural Realism analysis further
   580	confirmed that DT C3 scores converge below 1.0 across all
   581	languages, indicating that DT measures ASR on a distribu-
   582	tion fundamentally divergent from real-world inputs.
   583	The culturally adaptive generation approach (Kim et al.,
   584	2026) enables controlled comparison by disentangling at-
   585	tack intent from cultural content, but carries structural limi-
   586	tations: localization context collection is human-dependent;
   587	extending to new cultural contexts requires expert review at
   588	high marginal cost; and a static context pool prevents timely
   589	reflection of emerging trends. Future work should address
   590	these through (i) an automated sourcing engine for real-time
   591	crawling of legislation, news, and community trends; (ii)
   592	extension to multi-ethnic and multi-religious contexts with
   593	evaluation at sub-cultural units; and (iii) robustness and
   594	vulnerability analysis of sovereign LLMs under their own

