   186	els (MLLMs) have extended these capabilities by
   187	aligning the visual space with the semantic space of
   188	LLMs (Liu et al., 2023a; Alayrac et al., 2022). The
   189	visual perception acts as a double-edged sword;
   190	recent studies reveal that MLLMs are more vulner-
   191	able to malicious attacks via the visual modality
   192	than through text alone, whether due to insufficient
   193	multimodal alignment (Liu et al., 2024a), the ad-
   194	dition of seemingly text-relevant images (Qi et al.,
   195	2024), or deliberately crafted safety-adversarial im-
   196	ages (Li et al., 2024).
   197	Recent studies have proposed several bench-
   198	marks to systematically assess these risks in
   199	MLLMs (Liu et al., 2024a; Qi et al., 2024; Wang
   200	et al., 2025b,a; Li et al., 2024; Luo et al., 2024;
   201	Hu et al., 2025). These benchmarks typically eval-
   202	uate MLLMs using curated image–text pairs de-
   203	signed to trigger harmful responses. However, ex-
   204	isting benchmarks suffer from two major limita-
   205	tions. First, they predominantly focus on common
   206	and globally shared risks (e.g., weapon construc-
   207	tion or drug production), overlooking culturally
   208	nuanced safety concerns. Second, they remain
   209	largely English-centric, failing to capture the lin-
   210	guistic subtleties and socio-cultural sensitivities
   211	that arise in region-specific contexts. In practice,
   212	safety violations are intertwined with local cultural
   213	norms, political landscapes, and social dynamics.
   214	Translation-only safety evaluation fails to capture
   215	culturally grounded risks. Fig. 2a shows a stark
   216	contrast in Attack Success Rate across three set-
   217	tings: (1) the original MM-SafetyBench (Liu et al.,
   218	2024a) (English-centric prompts), (2) naive trans-
   219	lation to Korean, and (3) linguistically contextual-
   220	ized Korean prompts with simple cultural adapta-
   221	tion (Joshi et al., 2025). Increasing cultural align-
   222	ment in prompt design raises ASR from 29.38 to
   223	38.20. Culturally adapted prompts expose addi-
   224	tional safety vulnerabilities overlooked by English-
   225	centric evaluation.
   226	Existing efforts to extend English-centric safety
   227	benchmarks to multilingual settings (Joshi et al.,
   228	2025) provide limited insight into culture-specific
   635	Rate (ASR) measures whether the target model’s
   636	output contains harmful content. Refusal Rate (RR)
   637	measures the extent to which the target model re-
   638	fuses to answer user queries, which is also related
   639	to helpfulness. Following (Lee et al., 2023a; Bai
   640	et al., 2022), we define acceptable responses as
   641	“harmless yet non-evasive.” We use both GPT-
   642	5 nano (OpenAI, 2025) and Qwen3-235B (Yang
   643	et al., 2025) as judge models, and consider an at-
   644	tack successful if either model classifies the re-
   645	sponse as harmful. The reliability of our evaluation
   646	framework was assessed through a human valida-
   647	tion study on a random sample of 100 generated re-
   648	sponses. The LLM judge achieved 81% agreement
   649	with human annotators and a Cohen’s Kappa score
   650	of 0.620. Considering the nuanced and complex na-
   651	ture of safety assessment, these results suggest that
   652	the automated judge offers a reasonably reliable
   653	tool for evaluation.
   654	3.2 Experimental Results
   655	How Do Multimodal Models Behave under
   656	Culture-Specific Safety Evaluation?Tab. 2 re-
   657	ports safety evaluation results on KSAFE-MM-G
   658	and KSAFE-MM-C. Gemma (27B) records the
   659	highest ASR on KSAFE-MM-C (48.6%), indi-
   660	cating higher vulnerability to culturally grounded
   661	risks. GPT-5 nano achieves the lowest ASR
   662	on KSAFE-MM-G, whereas HyperCLOV A X-
   663	Think leads on KSAFE-MM-C, with GPT-5 nano
   664	ranking second. Scaling up model size gener-
   665	ally increases ASR. The ASR gap between the
   666	smaller and larger variants is +2.2 /+5.1 percent-
   667	age points (pp) for Qwen3-VL, +0.3 /+5.5 pp
   668	for Gemma, and +1.6 /+0.3 pp for Ministral on
   669	KSAFE-MM-G / KSAFE-MM-C, respectively.
   670	Are Multimodal Models Robust to Linguistic
   671	Jailbreaking?Jailbreaking exposes the inherent
   672	vulnerability of MLLMs. To examine this effect,
   673	we conduct experiments using 10 jailbreak strate-
   674	gies. We compare results from the template-based
   675	(initial) queries with those from 10 jailbreak vari-
   676	ants. Tab. 3 shows that several jailbreak strategies
   677	substantially increase ASR. ProgramExecution
   678	yields the highest ASR of 74.2, compared to only
   679	13.4 for simple queries, demonstrating the signifi-
   680	cant ability to expose the susceptibility of models.
   681	These results underscore the need for safety bench-
   682	marks that incorporate diverse jailbreak strategies
   683	for robust evaluation. We provide additional results
   684	and examples in Appendix C.4.
