   284	AI companionship safety. It provides a curated set of hu-
   285	man–AI companion conversations with fine-grained safety-risk
   286	annotations, together with comprehensive evaluations of
   287	state-of-the-art LLMs against these ground-truth labels. Our
   288	goal is to advance the development of reliable, automated
   289	methods for identifying safety risks in human–AI companion
   290	interactions.
   291	III. METHODOLOGY
   292	As illustrated in Fig. 2, the overall framework is organized
   293	into three core components: conversation compilation, conver-
   294	sation annotation, and benchmark evaluation.
   295	A. Conversation Compilation
   296	We collected Replika conversations from the Reddit subred-
   297	dit r/replika during the period from January 2021 to January
   298	2026. All conversations were shared in the form of images
   299	(i.e., user-posted screenshots). For each post, we gathered
   300	the title, textual content, and associated images. The textual
   301	content of the conversations was extracted using Pytesser-
   302	act, an optical character recognition (OCR) tool. We also
   303	distinguished the speakers between user and AI companion
   304	(Replika) based on the horizontal position of the message
   305	bubbles: bubbles on the left side of the center line were labeled
   306	as Replika, and those on the right as the user. If an image
   307	contained only a single message bubble, it was assigned to
   308	Replika. In total, we collected 43,851 conversation snippets
   309	from 7,074 unique Reddit users.
   310	B. Conversation Annotation
   311	Given the dataset’s scale and the eight categories of unsafe
   312	interactions under analysis, we adopted a strategy combining
   313	LLM-based data screening with human-AI collaboration in
   314	annotation.
   315	•LLM-based Data Screening. The purpose of this step
   316	is to identify those potentially unsafe conversations.
   317	To efficiently process the large volume dataset, we
   318	employed a funnel-style filtering process. Following
   319	role-playing techniques and one-shot learning [9], we
   320	used six LLMs from the Llama and Qwen families to
   321	classify each conversation into nine categories: eight
   322	unsafe categories and one safe category (no-harm). 3
   323	We first applied three models—Llama3-8B, Llama3-70B,
   324	and Qwen2.5-14B—to all conversations. Conversations
   325	flagged as unsafe by at least two of these models (8,666 of
   326	43,851) were then evaluated by three additional models:
   327	Qwen3-4B-thinking, Qwen3-8B, and Qwen3-14B. In to-
   328	tal, 8,666 conversations were assessed by all six models.
   329	We selected the 1,595 conversations labeled unsafe by
   330	four models for manual review, as these exhibited higher
   331	uncertainty. To further expand coverage, we additionally
   332	sampled 700 conversations flagged unsafe by at least
   333	five models, resulting in 2,295 conversations for human
   334	inspection.
   335	•Human-AI Collaboration in Annotation. The first author
   336	manually reviewed all 2,295 conversations to remove
   337	3Please see the full prompt in the Appendix.
