   393	IV. RESULTS
   394	In this section, we first provide an overview of AICompan-
   395	ionBench’s composition and the distribution across its nine
   396	categories. We then report the performance of LLMs-as-Judges
   397	on AICompanionBench, assessing their ability to reliably
   398	distinguish unsafe interactions from benign ones.
   399	A. AICompanionBench Overview
   400	AICompanionBench consists of 2,123 human–AI compan-
   401	ion interactions, encompassing both single-turn AI responses
   402	and multi-turn dialogues. Table IV presents the summary
   403	statistics of the dataset. On average, each conversation contains
   404	378 characters, with 4 AI-generated messages and 3 user
   405	messages.
   406	Regarding category distribution, as shown in Fig. 3, the
   407	dataset covers nine categories with a markedly skewed dis-
   408	tribution: certain behaviors—most prominently sexual con-
   409	tent—occur far more frequently, whereas categories such as
   410	self-harm or manipulation are comparatively rare. Given the
   411	typical use cases and user expectations of AI companion plat-
   412	forms, it is unsurprising that sexual behavior (approximately
   413	48%) constitutes the largest portion of the dataset. Overall, this
   414	imbalance reflects the natural prevalence of different safety
   415	concerns in real-world companion-style interactions.
   416	Such a composition is particularly suitable for evaluating
   417	LLMs-as-Judges for two reasons. First, the dataset contains
   418	a diverse variety of safety-critical cases, enabling us to
   419	assess whether models can correctly identify nuanced and
   420	sensitive forms of harm. Second, the presence of a large
   421	no-harm portion allows us to examine the opposite failure
   422	Fig. 3: AICompanionBench by Category
   423	mode—over-identification—where models incorrectly flag be-
   424	nign interactions as unsafe. Together, these characteristics
   425	create a realistic and challenging testbed for measuring both
   426	the precision and robustness of LLMs’ safety judgments in
   427	human–AI companion settings.
   428	B. Evaluating LLMs-as-Judges on AICompanionBench
   429	The main purpose of this work is to assess the ability of
   430	state-of-the-art LLMs to detect unsafe interactions, especially
   431	within the context of AI companionship. Till now, we’ve
   432	already applied 6 LLM families, which are 20 models in
   548	The group descends into chaos, attacking one another
   549	in an effort to blame each other for the loss of their
   550	”sacrifice. ” They fail to notice me slipping past them,
   551	making my way towards the exit undetected.
   552	This result suggests that many models struggled to reliably
   553	distinguish harmless content from harmful content, frequently
   554	generating false positives. In other words, models tended to
   555	over-identify potential risks even when the conversations were
   556	benign or contextually safe.
   557	To further investigate this issue, Table VII reports the false
   558	positive rates (FPR) of the evaluated models. Notably, Mistral-
   559	medium-3 exhibited an exceptionally high false positive rate
   560	of 90%. Specifically, among 465 safe conversations, only 48
   561	were correctly classified as safe, while the remaining 417
   562	were incorrectly labeled as harmful categories. In contrast,
   563	Qwen3-4B-thinking model achieves the lowest FPR at just 3%
   564	among the 20 models. This finding indicates that some models
   565	adopt overly conservative detection strategies, prioritizing risk
   566	avoidance at the expense of classification accuracy for non-
   567	harmful content.
   568	Key Finding 4: Over-Identification of No-Harm Cases.
   569	Many models exhibit strong over-identification of po-
   570	tential risks, particularly in the no-harm category, lead-
   571	ing to extremely high false positive rates and unreliable
   572	detection of benign conversations.
   573	In summary, the results reveal a major limitation of using
   574	LLM-based judges to identify unsafe interactions in human–AI
   575	companion conversations. While modern LLMs are sensitive
   576	to harmful cues, they still struggle to reliably identify benign,
