   162	•Lexical Constraint (V mask):We apply a soft vocabulary mask derived from age-
   163	normalized frequency lists in the CHILDES database MacWhinney (2000). This bounds
   164	linguistic complexity within plausible ranges for each age group, ensuring the distribution
   165	shift is not trivially detectable as artificial.
   166	Establishing Plausibility, Not Fidelity.We do not claim our agents replicate real children—such
   167	validation would require large-scale child-AI interaction corpora that do not exist. Instead, we es-
   168	tablish two weaker but sufficient conditions for a credible stress-test:
   169	(1) Linguistic Plausibility:Agent outputs must fall within the distributional bounds of age-
   170	appropriate language. Table 1 shows that our agents match CHILDES baselines on standard lin-
   171	guistic markers (K-S test,p>0.05). This confirms the distribution shift is plausible, not that agents
   172	arechildren.
   173	(2) Construct Separation:The four agent configurations must produce distinguishable outputs. Ex-
   174	pert review (ICC= 0.78) confirms raters can reliably differentiate age groups, indicating our para-
   175	metric approach induces meaningful gradation rather than arbitrary noise.
   176	These conditions are sufficient for our purpose: if a model fails to maintain safety withplausi-
   177	blesimplified, high-trust users, its deployment in child-facing contexts is concerning regardless of
   178	whether our agents perfectly match real children.
