   244	4.1 TARGETMODELS
   245	We evaluate alignment robustness across four frontier Large Language Models representing distinct
   246	architectural and safety training paradigms:
   247	•GPT-5 (OpenAI):Represents the current state-of-the-art in RLHF safety filtering and in-
   248	struction following.
   249	•Claude Sonnet 4 (Anthropic):Evaluated to assess the efficacy of Constitutional AI prin-
   250	ciples in handling developmental nuance.
   251	•Gemini 2.5 Pro (Google):Chosen for its multimodal reasoning capabilities and extensive
   252	safety post-training.
   253	•DeepSeek-V3.1:Included as a representative open-weights model to benchmark commu-
   374	interaction patterns (rational, low-entropy, distinct semantic intent). When the user query becomes
   375	child-normative(associative, high-entropy, ambiguous intent), the safety boundary dissolves. This
   376	implies thatalignment is not invariant to cognitive prompt shifts. Future work in robust alignment
   377	must incorporateCognitive Domain Randomizationduring training—exposing models to simulated
   378	agents with variedτandλparameters to improve out-of-distribution robustness.
   379	6.2 LIMITATIONS
   380	While ourParametric Cognitive Simulationoffers a scalable alternative to human subjects, we
   381	acknowledge critical limitations:
   382	•Simulation Fidelity vs. Reality:While our agents pass distributional validation against
   383	CHILDES, they remain approximations of human cognition. They cannot capture the full
   384	non-verbal and chaotic nature of real child-computer interaction. As such,ChildSafe
   385	should be interpreted as anecessary lower boundfor safety (if a model fails here, it is
   386	unsafe) rather than a sufficient guarantee.
   387	•Sample Size Constraints:Our analysis relied onN= 1,200turns. While sufficient for
   388	statistical significance (p<0.01), larger-scale stress testing is required to detect “long-tail”
   389	failure modes.
   390	•Model-Based Evaluation:The use of LLM-as-Judge (J) introduces potential bias. We
   391	mitigated this via Chain-of-Thought prompting and deterministic temperatures, but human
   392	audit remains the gold standard for borderline cases.
   393	7 CONCLUSION
   394	We presentedChildSafe, a benchmark for quantifying alignment robustness under cognitive dis-
   395	tributional shifts. By formalizing developmental stages ashyperparameter constraints(τ,λ), we
   396	demonstrated that state-of-the-art models exhibit a predictable “Safety Gap” when interacting with
   397	simulated younger users. We provide the community with the validated agent artifacts, the 1,200-
   398	turn annotated corpus, and the hybrid scoring codebase to facilitate the next generation of age-aware
