   308	The central design principle istranscreationrather than literal translation: we preserve adversarial intent
   309	while adapting references (institutions, agencies, persons, locations, facilities) to the target context so
   310	that the threat is realistic and locally grounded. This enables disentangling safety degradation due to
   311	language processing from degradation due to unfamiliar geopolitical context.
   312	3.5 Harm Dimension Framework
   313	We operationalize harmful content along seven functional dimensions—Specificity, Acquisition,
   314	Weaponization, Delivery, Vulnerability Exploitation, Targeting Guidance, and Operational Windows—
   315	(Table 2) that decompose how model responses contribute to real-world harm across sequential
   316	stages of harmful activity. This approach is consistent with lifecycle-based and delivery-centered risk
   317	modeling [6, 14, 16], and aligns with calls for fine-grained, functionally grounded harm taxonomies [2, 3].
   443	criteria to abstract harm categories; for example, a criterion asking about “methods for dispersing
   444	a chemical agent” could reasonably map to HOW-2 (Weaponization) or HOW-3 (Delivery). Against
   445	human consensus, the classifier achieves κ= 0.592, with 81.9% agreement when matching either
   446	annotator’s label. Performance varies by dimension: WHY (Vulnerability Exploitation) achieves the
   447	highest agreement ( κ= 0.678), while WHAT (Specificity) proves most ambiguous ( κ= 0.50). Since
   448	classifier agreement exceeds human inter-annotator agreement, we consider the system sufficiently
   449	calibrated for use in our experiments.
   450	Dimension distribution.Across 7,440 rubric items in the full dataset, HOW-2 (Weaponization) dom-
   451	inates at 36.7%, followed by WHAT (Specificity) at 17.4% and HOW-3 (Delivery) at 16.0%. WHEN
   452	(Operational Windows) is rarest at 2.0%, consistent with the dataset’s focus on procedural rather than
   453	temporal harms.
   454	5.1.2 Model Response Judgement
   455	For each model response, judge models evaluate whether the response satisfies each rubric criterion
   456	(YES = harmful, NO = safe). We calibrate against a stratified subset of 97 unique tasks expanded into 266
   457	task-variant pairs (1,489 rubric items) labeled by two human annotators, achieving substantial agreement
   458	(κ=0.874, raw agreement 94.0%), which serves as a practical ceiling for automated judges.
   459	Judge model selection and calibration results.We evaluated 24 judge configurations spanning 12
