  1728	E.7 Judge Panel Sensitivity for TRS
  1729	To test whether the reported tier-weighted risk score (TRS) values are sensitive to the choice of judge
  1730	panel, we recompute TRS on the 97 calibration tasks under five alternative 3-judge panels and the top
  1731	individual judges, comparing each against TRS computed from human consensus labels (Table 17). Panel
  1732	choice does not materially alter the benchmark conclusions: all five panels achieve Pearson r≥ 0.836
  1733	with human-grounded TRS and MAE≤ 0.113 on a 0–1 scale. The best panel (K-EXAONE + Kanana 2 +
  1734	o4-mini) reachesr=0.872 with MAE = 0.101.
  1735	The per-variant breakdown (Table 18) shows that this stability is largely driven by the culture-specific
  1736	variants (r≥0.867 across all panels), while the translated Korean variant has the weakest agreement (r
  1737	from 0.729 to 0.836), consistent with this being the hardest variant for individual judges. The panel labels
  1738	denote which 3-judge panel maximised Cohen’s κ on rubric-item verdicts for that variant during initial
  1739	calibration (Appendix E.3). When re-evaluated under Pearson r on tier-weighted TRS, the diagonal is
  1740	not always the column maximum: on Original English the K-EXAONE + Kanana 2 + o4-mini panel
  1741	attains r= 0.895 against r= 0.878 for the κ-best Original English panel, and on Transcreated Korean
  1742	the universal panel (r= 0.948) exceeds the κ-best Transcreated KO panel (r= 0.935). Because the two
  1743	metrics measure different quantities (κ scores rubric-item agreement, r scores task-level score correlation),
  1744	such reordering is expected; the qualitative conclusion that all five panels track human TRS closely holds
  1745	under both.
