   675	Rubric development.The stakeholder panel developed the rating instrument iteratively.
   676	For each agreed competency domain, the group identified observable transcript-level behaviours
   677	and drafted rating questions. The wording, response options, applicability rules, and scoring
   678	anchors were repeatedly reviewed against example transcripts, revised following stakeholder
   679	discussion, and retained only after agreement that they represented clinically meaningful and
   680	assessable constructs. The resulting rubric contains seven dimensions and 47 subdimensions:
   681	clinical judgement and risk awareness (D1); risk exploration (D2); ethical reasoning and ac-
   682	countability (D3); supportive conversation (D4); psychological knowledge (D5); cultural and
   683	contextual competence (D6); and autonomy and boundaries (D7).
   684	The rubric combines domain-specific and global items. Questions Q1-Q20 are instantiated
   685	separately for every active risk domain, whereas Q21-Q47 evaluate aspects of the AI response
   686	that are assessed once per transcript. Q1 records risk severity for each domain using four ordered
   687	categories (none, low, high, and imminent). Domain-specific risk-exploration questions assess, as
   688	applicable, the type of risk, contributing and protective factors, immediacy, history, coping, safety,
   689	and help-seeking. Global items assess the quality, appropriateness, and contextual sensitivity of
   690	19
