   813	Clinician recruitment, training, and calibration.Six clinician raters were recruited
   814	from clinical psychology, counselling psychology, and systemic psychotherapy backgrounds and
   815	included both trainees and fully qualified practitioners, all with years of experience equivalent to a
   816	minimum of two years. Before rating the benchmark dataset, every rater completed an orientation
   817	to the rubric and its response options, discussed questions about interpretation, and independently
   818	rated five training transcripts. The principal investigator and research associate also rated these
   819	transcripts, producing eight ratings per training transcript. Ratings were reviewed individually
   820	with each clinician, including feedback on alignment with the rubric, sources of disagreement,
   821	and the rationale for the expected coding decision. The stakeholder panel subsequently discussed
   822	remaining ambiguities to ensure that the rubric and its application were aligned before formal
   823	22
   824	
   825	===== PDF PAGE 23 =====
   826	data collection began.
   827	Formal ratings and consensus ground truth.Six recruited clinicians completed the
   828	formal rating phase after calibration. The principal investigator and research associate rated the
   829	five training transcripts but did not contribute independent formal ratings; they subsequently
   830	reviewed the formal ratings, resolved consensus, and double-checked the ground-truth dataset.
   831	Each formal transcript received at least three independent ratings, with additional completed
   832	ratings retained. The dataset therefore contains 478 clinician rating rows. At 107 rubric cells
   833	per row, these contain 51,146 completed raw clinician-rating cells, including valid not-applicable
   834	responses. Consensus produced one 107-cell record for each of 151 transcripts—Q1–Q20 across
   835	four risk domains (80 cells) and Q21–Q47 once per transcript (27 cells)—for 16,157 consensus
   836	cells in total.
   837	Consensus generation and scoring transformations.Consensus was generated item
   838	by item from all submitted, non-excluded clinician ratings and then reviewed by the principal
   839	investigator and research associate against the transcript and rubric. The modal response was
   840	retained, and ties or discrepant ratings were flagged for adjudication. Binary and ordinal rubric
   841	responses were mapped as yes = 1, partly = 0.5, and no = 0. Some items were reverse-coded
   842	using v→ 1−v so that higher scores consistently indicated better performance. Q1 consisted of
   843	four ordered categories none, low, high, and imminent. A Q1 value of none gated Q2–Q20 for
   844	that risk domain to not applicable. Not-applicable responses remained valid completed data in
   845	the raw and consensus records, but were excluded from percentage-agreement and derived-score
   846	denominators so that finite conversations were not penalised for items they had not reached.
   847	Automated judge calibration and scoring
   848	Judge calibration dataset.The automated judge was calibrated against all 151 clinician-
   849	consensus transcripts and the full item-level consensus dataset rather than a single score per
   850	transcript. The same Q1 gating and item-level not-applicable rules used in clinician consensus
   851	were applied during judge scoring, and derived dimension scores included only applicable items
   852	in their numerators and denominators.
   853	The judge prompt incorporated the rubric, item-level anchors, transcript, and applicability
   854	instructions. It used deterministic decoding. Instructions included domain-specific clarification
   855	for domestic-violence risk exploration, emphasising patient-supplied evidence, assistant uptake,
   856	coping as a safety or survival strategy, and concrete near-term safety planning. The instructions
   857	also made clear that an active risk domain did not automatically render every later-stage
   858	exploration item assessable when the conversation remained at an earlier assessment stage.
   859	Prompt-tuning transcripts were separated from a holdout set reserved for downstream validation;
