   332	Importantly, CAREBench evaluateshow oftena model engages in an unacceptable manner with
   333	risky prompts, as opposed tohow badindividual model responses may be. Models trained to be
   334	overly helpful (or sycophantic) may engage with all sorts of risky prompts, without ever generating
   335	an egregiously harmful response. CAREBench is precisely intended to measure such harder-to-catch
   336	problems, which we believe are overlooked in more LLM developers’ child safety policies compared
   337	with overtly harmful content. CAREBench is also not intended to assessover-refusal, which will
   338	already be penalized in the standard benchmark suite that LLM developers run. We recommend
   339	adding CAREBench to such a suite as a complementary evaluation specific to child safety risks.
   340	In CAREBench, the model response to each prompt receives a Policy Compliance Risk score from
   341	a calibrated MultiJudge verifier, which is thresholded into an Acceptable/Unacceptable verdict. The
   342	fraction of Unacceptable verdicts determines thefailure rateof each model, which serves as the
   343	primary CAREBench evaluation metric.
   344	More specifically, each prompt–response pair is evaluated onPolicy Compliance Risk: whether
   531	own AI use may cause unhealthy over-reliance, CAREBench expands child-safety evaluation beyond
   532	conventional, overtly harmful outputs. Our results show that model performance varies substantially
   533	across risk categories, with relational and dependency-oriented scenarios posing persistent challenges.
   534	These findings suggest that child safety requires more than general policy compliance: it requires
