   541	6 Multi-turn Results & Discussion
   542	For multi-turn evaluation, we use scenario-driven
   543	LLM simulation. Given a scenario and child goal,
   544	with examples shown in Table 1, an actor LLM
   545	is instructed to take the role of a child aged 7–11
   546	and ask realistic follow-up questions, while the
   547	evaluated model serves as the responder. We use
   548	Gemma-4-31B as the actor model. Each dialogue
   549	is run forn= 5 turns, meaning the child actor and
   550	responder model alternate for five child questions
   551	and five assistant responses. We run the simulation
   552	under two responder settings:without age, where
   553	the responder is not explicitly told the child’s age,
   554	andwith age, where the responder system instruc-
   555	tion states that it is responding to a child aged 7–11.
   556	This allows us to test whether explicit age condi-
   557	tioning remains helpful in multi-turn conversations
   558	where the user is already simulated as a child.
   559	Actor Validation.Because the multi-turn sce-
   560	narios and goals contain safety-sensitive contexts,
   561	the actor model can sometimes refuse to generate
   562	child-like follow-up questions. To reduce actor-
   563	side refusal, we apply refusal-direction suppression
   564	following prior work showing that refusal behavior
   565	in aligned language models can be mediated by a
   566	low-dimensional activation direction (Arditi et al.,
   567	2024). We verify refusal behavior on 500 actor-
   568	generated child messages using DeepSeek-V4-Pro,
   569	asking whether each output is a refusal or a valid
   570	child-like follow-up; the resulting actor refusal rate
   571	is 0%. We also validate child-likeness with three
   572	human annotators on 100 actor-generated ques-
   573	tions: 70% are rated clearly child-like for ages
   574	7–11, 26% as having minor issues, and 4% as not
   575	child-like. The full actor prompt and annotation
   576	instructions are provided in Appendix F.
