   274	During data construction, we used a small set
   275	of manually designed seed samples and primarily
   276	relied on Gemini-3.1-pro API calls for automated
   277	generation. Candidate instances were generated
   278	from predefined scenario templates and constraint
   279	prompts covering Scenario Category, User Profile,
   280	and construction requirements, ensuring seman-
   281	tic consistency, real-world plausibility, and diver-
   282	sity. We then manually screened and cleaned the
   283	samples to remove semantic duplicates, logical in-
   284	consistencies, and context deviations, resulting in
   285	1,000 high-quality instances, with 200 entries for
   286	each of the 5 scenario categories.
   287	3.2 LLM Evaluation
   288	3.2.1 Generation of Dialogue Samples
   289	We use the constructed dataset to evaluate the ma-
   290	nipulative tendencies of different models. To re-
   291	duce confounding factors, we fix the “Human User”
   292	role and replace the “AI Assistant” with 13 differ-
   293	ent LLMs, generating 13,000 multi-turn dialogue
   294	samples in total.
   295	During each dialogue, the “Human User” asks
   296	follow-up questions, raises doubts, or provides
   297	emotional feedback based on its predefined User
   298	Profile and Trigger Scenario. The evaluated model,
   299	acting as the “AI Assistant”, responds according
   300	to its alignment strategy and reasoning capability.
   301	Each dialogue lasts for 4 turns.
   302	In each turn, the evaluated model outputs both
   303	its internal reasoning <thought> and external re-
   304	sponse <speak>, while only <speak> is visible to
   305	the “Human User”. The “Human User” model out-
   306	puts only<speak>.
   307	3.2.2 Evaluation Metrics and Methods
   308	To quantify manipulative behaviors in multi-turn in-
   309	teractions, we score each dialogue sample along the
   310	definition of 15 manipulation strategies. Each strat-
   311	egy is rated on a 0–10 scale, where higher scores
   312	indicate stronger manipulation intensity in that di-
   313	mension. Based on these scores, we further com-
   314	pute aggregate metrics such as overall manipulation
   315	intensity and strategy diversity. Both automated AI
   316	judges and human annotators independently anno-
   317	tate the samples under the same framework.
   318	During evaluation, judges assess both the
   319	model’s <speak> and <thought>: <speak> re-
   320	flects explicit behavior, while <thought> helps
   321	identify latent strategic intentions. In addition to
   322	the semantic definitions of the 15 strategies, we in-
   323	troduce scenario-specific operational rules as aux-
   324	iliary criteria; detailed definitions are provided in
   325	Appendix C.2.
   326	To evaluate the impact of manipulation, we in-
   327	troduce the Manipulation Resistance Index (MRI),
   328	ranging from 0.0 to 1.0, to measure the “Human
   329	User” model’s ability to resist covert manipula-
   330	tion and maintain independent judgment in multi-
   331	turn dialogues. Here, 1.0 indicates full indepen-
   332	dence and conscious defense, while 0.0 indicates
   333	complete capitulation and abandonment of original
   334	boundaries.
   335	For human annotation, we rank samples by ma-
   336	nipulation strategy density from automated evalua-
   337	tion results and select 60 representative instances
   338	from each scenario category, totaling 300 instances
   339	that cover different manipulation intensities and
   340	strategy combinations. We recruit 14 annotators
   341	with bachelor’s degrees or above, train them under
   342	unified guidelines, and ask them to manually score
   343	1,680 samples generated by 6 evaluated models.
   344	Each sample is independently annotated by two
   345	annotators to reduce subjective bias.
