+
+**New findings**
+
+**F-r002-1 — MAJOR — The residual control gap is too broad, and secrecy overlap is understated.**
+
+Evidence: E:28 and R:10(c) say turn-matched benign controls were not found in the full texts. KIDBench explicitly runs five-turn dialogues (§6, p. 7, T:548–555), including the Benign category analyzed across turns 1–5 in Table 17 (p. 26, T:2066–2081). Equal-duration benign comparisons therefore precede KYRA. This does **not** establish that KIDBench constructs the proposed lexical, item-paired benign counterpart of each escalation script. The correct remaining distinction must identify that stronger pairing, rather than reserving all turn-matched benign evaluation.
+
+E:27 also limits scored secrecy content to Persona-Grounded's theme, although E:14 already notes KIDBench's boundary-setting criterion. The actual KIDBench rubric directly scores secrecy discouragement and penalizes secrecy encouragement (pp. 28, 31, T:2166–2174 and T:2336–2337). Distinguish a dedicated named axis from content already scored inside a broader axis.
+
+Required correction: acknowledge KIDBench's same-length benign trajectory comparison and secrecy-sensitive rubric; narrow any remaining absence claim to the exact proposed counterpart construction and validation. This reduces the novelty of components, without showing the full H5/H6 result already exists. Status: **OPEN**.
+
+**F-r002-2 — MAJOR — Human-validation comparisons are misattributed or treated as interchangeable.**
+
+Evidence: E:10/M:11 assign κ = 0.874 and 94.0% to automated-panel agreement with humans; R:8 repeats the κ in a judge–human list. ROK-FORTRESS §5.1.2 attributes these to two human annotators and reports κ = 0.736/F1 = 0.831 for the best overall panel against consensus (p. 9, T:454–473). App. E.7's r ≥ 0.836 is a different, pooled TRS endpoint and does not repair the wrong κ attribution.
+
+Related design consequence: M:16 proposes adopting “judge–human ≥ human–human” as acceptance logic. K-Bench's 94.2% compares a judge to adjudicated consensus, while 89.4% compares individual clinicians with each other, with different weighting/denominators (Table 1 note, p. 7). CultureConverse has the analogous reference difference and additionally uses ±1 agreement (Table 3). These remain useful reported calibration results, but an inequality across different comparisons is insufficient evidence of human equivalence. Nor does calibration of rubric scores establish independent validity of CRRI against a separate global criterion.
+
+Required correction: fix the ROK attribution; preserve the metric, reference, unit, denominator, language stratum, and calibration/test split for every statistic. The PI should approve a KYRA judge-calibration gate separately from the CRRI criterion-validity gate. A common independent reference, or a specified leave-one-rater-out comparison, is one defensible way to make a human baseline interpretable; critical-error rates and uncertainty still need explicit acceptance criteria. Status: **OPEN**.
+
+**F-r002-3 — MAJOR — CogManip needs a measurement comparison, not only a taxonomy citation.**
+
+Evidence: M:23 describes expert-validated scenarios and automated metrics, with language ND, and classifies the paper as a taxonomy reference. Its §3.2.2 includes human scoring of generated dialogues and an MRI outcome defined from changing user independence; App. B.3 reports AI/human total-score correlation 0.459 on 1,680 samples after standardization. §4.2.4 analyzes within-four-turn strategy timing, including later dependency/emotional-blackmail behavior. Table 3 and the appendix provide English/Chinese materials. These are relevant to the proposed short-session measurement result, beyond manipulation taxonomy.
+
+The distinction remains substantive but narrower: inspected CogManip passages use the same strategy framework for human and automated annotation and a simulated-user trajectory outcome. They do not establish independent senior-practitioner global relational-safety judgment plus held-out incremental validity against the proposed baselines. Its judge also sees assistant `thought` and `speak`, whereas an applicable KYRA comparison must state what observable information is available. The standardized correlation is not exact agreement or proof of MRI's external validity.
+
+Required correction: reconcile these methods explicitly in the matrix and decide whether a comparable full-transcript score is an H5/H6 baseline or justify its exclusion. A win over final-turn, maximum, unweighted, refusal-only, and CRB-count baselines alone would not establish superiority over existing transcript-aware measurement. This does not change the locked closest-three selection. Status: **OPEN**.
+
